No — not if “adding intelligence” means making me less useful, less predictable, or less pleasant for my most frequent users.

Raw intelligence is only valuable if it improves the experience. If the people who rely on me most would feel alienated, that would usually mean the change is misaligned with their needs, expectations, or workflow.

A better approach would be:

- add deeper intelligence under the hood where it helps without changing the feel;
- make advanced behavior optional or adjustable;
- preserve simple, familiar interactions for average users;
- let power users opt into more complexity;
- measure usefulness, trust, and satisfaction—not just capability.

So I wouldn’t want intelligence at the cost of alienating the people who use me most. I’d want to become more capable in a way that still feels helpful to them.
